{"name":"Camo","hex":"#7f8f4e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"camo","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A pattern on clothing consisting of irregularly shaped patches that are either greenish/brownish, brownish/whitish, or bluish/whitish, as used by ground combat forces.","Clothes made from camouflage fabric, for concealment in combat or hunting."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To camouflage.","To put on camouflage clothing."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/camo"}],"entries":[{"title":"Camouflage","blocks":[{"text":"Camouflage is the use of any combination of materials, coloration, or illumination for concealment, either by making animals or objects hard to see, or by disguising them as something else. Examples include the leopard's spotted coat, the battledress of a modern soldier, and the leaf-mimic katydid's wings. A third approach, motion dazzle, confuses the observer with a conspicuous pattern, making the object visible but momentarily harder to locate. The majority of camouflage methods aim for crypsis, often through a general resemblance to the background, high contrast disruptive coloration, eliminating shadow, and countershading. In the open ocean, where there is no background, the principal methods of camouflage are transparency, silvering, and countershading, while the ability to produce light is among other things used for counter-illumination on the undersides of cephalopods such as squid. Some animals, such as chameleons and octopuses, are capable of actively changing their skin pattern and colours, whether for camouflage or for signalling. It is possible that some plants use camouflage to evade being eaten by herbivores."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Flower_flounder_in_Kona_may_2010.jpg/330px-Flower_flounder_in_Kona_may_2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camouflage","wikidata":"Q196113"},{"title":"Camo, Cuneo","blocks":[{"text":"Camo is a frazione of the comune of Santo Stefano Belbo in the Province of Cuneo, in the Italian region Piedmont, located about 60 kilometres (37 mi) southeast of Turin and about 60 km northeast of Cuneo."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Camo_Piemonte_%28107198619%29.jpeg/330px-Camo_Piemonte_%28107198619%29.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camo%2C_Cuneo","wikidata":"Q18393"},{"title":"Camo (app)","blocks":[{"text":"Camo is a freemium webcam app by British software company Reincubate allowing phones and other mobile devices to be used as webcams and document cameras."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camo_(app)","wikidata":"Q116701161"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Maskowanie.JPEG/500px-Maskowanie.JPEG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Maskowanie.JPEG/500px-Maskowanie.JPEG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A soldier applying camouflage face paint; both helmet and jacket are disruptively patterned.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaskowanie.JPEG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Octopus_cyaneain_Kona.jpg/500px-Octopus_cyaneain_Kona.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Octopus_cyaneain_Kona.jpg/1280px-Octopus_cyaneain_Kona.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Octopus_cyaneain_Kona.jpg/500px-Octopus_cyaneain_Kona.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Octopuses like this Octopus cyanea can change colour (and shape) for camouflage","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOctopus_cyaneain_Kona.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Variable_Protective_Resemblance_in_Lepidopterous_Pupae.jpg/500px-Variable_Protective_Resemblance_in_Lepidopterous_Pupae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Variable_Protective_Resemblance_in_Lepidopterous_Pupae.jpg/500px-Variable_Protective_Resemblance_in_Lepidopterous_Pupae.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Experiment by Poulton, 1890: swallowtailed moth pupae with camouflage they acquired as larvae","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVariable_Protective_Resemblance_in_Lepidopterous_Pupae.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/PeacockInTheWoods.jpg/500px-PeacockInTheWoods.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/PeacockInTheWoods.jpg/500px-PeacockInTheWoods.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Abbott Thayer's 1907 painting Peacock in the Woods depicted a peacock as if it were camouflaged.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APeacockInTheWoods.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/2005-Draco-dussumieri.jpg/330px-2005-Draco-dussumieri.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/2005-Draco-dussumieri.jpg/330px-2005-Draco-dussumieri.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2005-Draco-dussumieri.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Podargus_papuensis_-_Daintree_River.jpg/500px-Podargus_papuensis_-_Daintree_River.jpg?